Subject: [PATCH v2 0/8] arm64 boot-time HugeTLB vmemmap optimization

Prepare shared vmemmap backing while reserving boot-configured arm64
gigantic HugeTLB pages, before ordinary struct-page population. The scope
is 4 KiB base pages and 1 GiB boot pages, using the existing
hugetlb_free_vmemmap=on control. Active HugeTLB CMA and PREEMPT_RT retain
their existing paths.

This local OLK adaptation uses deferred backing population and candidate
mapping preparation. It is not a direct backport of the community series.
It reuses the vendor arm64 BBM/fault-repair foundation rather than replacing
it with the newer mailing-list AF proposal.

Changes since the five-patch local v1:

- Enable per-page HVO protection before shared descriptor initialization
  and the first BBM update, retaining successful-restore key accounting.
- Retire backing and actual allocated page-table cost as candidates are
  prepared, rather than retaining a full ordinary metadata budget.
- Grant no backing credit on preparation failure; require ordinary
  fallback to fit or withdraw the entire unpublished reservation.
- Treat prospective-budget exhaustion as a short allocation, not a BUG.
- Prefer metadata fragments without bypassing mirrored-memory preference.
- Support high-address-space QEMU tests through RAM_RESERVE=on|off,
  preserving the default and the exact pool/lifecycle assertions.

The original five local commits are preserved, followed by three review
fixes. Apply all eight numbered patches on the stated base.

Validation includes arm64 39-bit and 48-bit VA builds, x86_64 SMP HVO with
boot optimization compiled out, and QEMU lifecycle, NUMA, demotion, HVO-off,
reservation and failure-path tests. The 128 GiB guest now provides the
requested 126-page pool, versus 125 on v1, and shrinks the full pool after
checking a mapped 1 GiB page. Sparse-RAM oversized requests and synthetic
mirrored-memory selection have failing-old/passing-new evidence. All
scratch injections were removed from the delivered source.

These results do not measure whole-system peak memory or validate physical
arm64 mirroring/BBM races. Four deliberate accounting/release/debug BUG
warnings remain in checkpatch; there are zero checkpatch errors for the
review-fix kernel diff. The final independent review approved that diff.

